<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maeda Tsunanori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1724">1724</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maeda Tsunanori was the fifth lord of the Kaga domain, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a> for most of the later 17th century. He was a noted patron of scholarship who collected and copied old books and documents, and he encouraged the crafts that made Kanazawa famous, including gold leaf, lacquer and Kutani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>. His reign is seen as the cultural high point of Kaga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maeda-tsunanori.jpg" alt="Maeda Tsunanori" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 下村観山筆 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maeda_Tsunanori.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maeda Tsunanori was born on 26 December 1643 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda clan</a> residence in Edo, the eldest son of Maeda Mitsutaka; his childhood name was Inuchiyo. His father died in 1645 at the age of 29, leaving the domain to a two-year-old, and by order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> his grandfather Maeda Toshitsune became regent. In 1654 he came of age in a genpuku ceremony over which Shōgun Tokugawa Ietsuna presided, receiving court rank and the title Kaga-no-kami.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1658 he was married for political reasons to Suma-hime, the ten-year-old daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshina-masayuki">Hoshina Masayuki</a> of Aizu Domain; she died in 1666 at 18, and he never formally remarried. The same year Toshitsune died, and at 15 Tsunanori took full control of the domain. He grouped villages in tens to ease tax collection and the opening of new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> land, put famine relief and medical care on a more systematic footing, and revised the domain's laws, which had been severe since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-toshiie">Maeda Toshiie</a>'s suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a>. He also settled a long border dispute with Fukui Domain over the holy mountain of Hakusan.</p><p>He patronised the arts, above all Noh performances, and built up a major collection of Japanese and Chinese literature, though he is best known for developing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenroku-en">Kenroku-en</a> gardens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>. In 1689, under Shōgun Tokugawa Tsunayoshi, the domain was granted the same courtesy at audiences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gosanke">Gosanke</a>, and its assessed yield was set at 1 million koku.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tsunanori had children by several concubines, and his daughters married into the Asano, Ikeda and Nijō families among others. He was raised to the court rank of Sangi in 1693 and to Senior Third Grade in 1707. He retired from public life in 1723 and died on 29 June 1724 at the age of 80. In 1909 he was posthumously raised to the Second Grade of court rank.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maeda Tsunanori” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Maeda Tsunanori</b> patronised scholarship and crafts, and Kanazawa became a centre of gold leaf, Kutani porcelain and Kaga yuzen dyeing, with the Kenroku-en garden.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaga-domain">Kaga Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1695" aria-label="Edo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8080 875 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maeda Tsunanori"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8530 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8530 1145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1695">What was here in 1695? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
